WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
When you write unit tests for iOS, X-test test operates inside a complete Apple process.

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.600
It boots up with full runtimes already linked and active.

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.960
Gest operates under completely different constraints.

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.000
It runs entirely inside Node.js.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.400
In Node, there is no UI kit.

6
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.880
There is no Core location, no Objective-C runtime, and no Java bridge.

7
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.640
On a real device, a JavaScript thread successfully crosses the bridge

8
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.720
to a populated native registry.

9
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.760
In Gest, it hits an empty, bed-end void.

10
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.160
Because this registry is completely empty,

11
00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.920
invoking a native counterpart guarantees an immediate type error.

12
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.680
To prevent this, you have to architect pure JavaScript standards

13
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.880
to replace every single native boundary in your code base.

14
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.600
This is what we call strict boundary interception.

15
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.960
It is the process of catching component imports and network calls

16
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.360
before they reach the missing native bridge.

17
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.080
Mocking ingest is a mechanical necessity.

18
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.280
Without intercepting these calls, your test suite will fail

19
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.080
with catastrophic null pointer exceptions.

20
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.880
The resolution cycle begins the moment you write a standard component import statement,

21
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.640
tracing down the dependency tree to find the required module.

22
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.520
Normally, when you're at boots on a physical device,

23
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.200
native C++ and Objective C++ implementations

24
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.280
populate the turbo-module registry during the hardware bootstrap phase.

25
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.480
Node completely bypasses this hardware bootstrap phase.

26
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.200
As a result, the native registry remains permanently empty during your test runs.

27
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.720
You intercept the cycle using the just.mox option.

28
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.200
By placing it at the very top of a test file,

29
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.920
it pre-empts the standard import resolution.

30
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.000
Here, just.mox acts as a barrier midway down the require cycle.

31
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.240
It halts the request before the native layer,

32
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.200
returning a new JavaScript factory object back up.

33
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.920
This ensures the test receives a safe pure JavaScript object preventing crashes.

34
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.360
This mechanism serves the exact same purpose as constructor injection and swift,

35
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.960
where you swap out a dependency before a test method executes.

36
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:07.120
Writing these mock factory functions manually across dozens of individual test files

37
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.720
quickly clutters your code base.

38
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.440
You solve this by creating a double underscore mox directory.

39
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.240
This establishes global automatic manual mox for any recurring dependencies.

40
00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:24.720
Intercepting the module resolution cleanly severs JavaScript from the absence C++ registry.

41
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.640
Your component is now clear to render in complete isolation.

42
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.080
Rendering a component is only the first step.

43
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.880
You also have to manage components that rely on local persistent state dependencies,

44
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.720
like React Native MMKV.

45
00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.800
MMKV is a native C++ storage module,

46
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.560
so it instantly fails ingest.

47
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.640
You must replicate its state mechanics entirely in JavaScript.

48
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.320
The most effective strategy is replacing the native storage module with a standard in-memory

49
00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:02.080
JavaScript map. This image displays the implementation. Left, the standard native MKVV initialization.

50
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:08.960
Right, our map replacement. Notice the highlighted before each block, calling store.clear,

51
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.320
preventing test pollution.

52
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.480
Next, you will need to evaluate conditional logic pathways that are gated by platform-specific

53
00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:23.120
constants, like iOS versus Android. If you attempt a direct assignment to platform.OS,

54
00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.040
modern React Native versions will throw a read-only execution error.

55
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.720
The only reliable method to override platform constants on a per-test basis is by utilizing

56
00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.320
object.define property. As your test suite grows, you will repeatedly construct these state and

57
00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.720
platform mock objects. Factory functions are required to manage this repetition.

58
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.320
Factory functions enforce strict type script interfaces. They catch type mismatches at

59
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.760
authoring time, long before the test actually runs. Keeping state and platform contacts strictly

60
00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.720
within JavaScript ensures your test suite remains perfectly deterministic, regardless of the

61
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.920
operating system running the test script. There is one final external boundary that will cause your

62
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:09.840
test to fail. Outbound network requests. In Native iOS development, you manage this with URL

63
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:16.880
protocol subclassing. In the just ecosystem, you use mock service worker, or MSW. MSW is not a

64
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:22.720
simple mock function. It installs itself directly as a node process level HTTP interceptor.

65
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.080
When your component makes a fetch request, a data packet moves outward. The MSW interceptor catches

66
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:33.120
it, cross references its own handler list, and immediately injects a synthetic JSON response

67
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.160
back into the execution thread, allowing JavaScript to continue unhindered. You can override a

68
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.520
successful handler on a per-test basis to return specific status codes, validating how your application

69
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:50.480
handles error states. To secure this boundary, always configure your MSW server with the on-unhandled

70
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:57.200
request parameter set to error. Forcing unstubbed requests to throw fatal errors, guarantees the

71
00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.720
network boundary remains hermetically sealed, preventing silent failures and flaky behavior during

72
00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.160
your CI pipelines. This code block represents a fully architected, production-ready,

73
00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.160
react native test file. It brings all of these interceptor layers together.

74
00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.400
At the top of the file, the MSW server configuration actively secures the network boundary against

75
00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.640
any outbound HTTP requests. Directly below that, the manual mock declarations sever the component

76
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.400
from all hardware and sensor dependencies. Finally, the render execution block operates entirely

77
00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:35.440
within standard JavaScript paradigms, safely isolated from the missing C++ registry.

78
00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:41.760
The component is now successfully executing with zero real GPS data, zero network connectivity,

79
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:48.080
and zero native modules. Just mocking is the strict engineering discipline of replacing dependencies

80
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:54.000
precisely at the platform boundary. By systematically intercepting modules and network layers before

81
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.680
they cross into the node void, you guarantee your unit tests remain fast, deterministic, and highly

82
00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.680
reliable.

